1vyrain installation on Lenovo Thinkpad X230

I'm not sure about you but sometimes I brush off my old Lenovo Thinkpad X230.

With this, I figure we should give it a bit of a refresh.

The Lenovo Thinkpad X230 is a magnificant device. Slightly dated now but nonetheless, have you heard about 1vyrain. It is a script that allows you to overclock your cpu, remove the wireless nic whitelist for your <specific> thinkpad, as well as, soft disable Intel ME + more.

I think that's pretty cool.

Therefore, let's give it a shot.

First things first. My device is a: Lenovo Thinkpad X230 and the bios that are currently supported sit at 2.60 and lower.

My laptop's BIOS was sitting at: 2.59.

So no downgrade is required.

Items required for procedure
- Flashdrive
- 1vyrain iso from https://1vyra.in/
- You meet the bios version to flash the custom bios

1. I downloaded the 1vyrain.iso from the 1vyrain website: https://1vyra.in/
2. Proceeded to set up a bootable flash media using the following command: 

sudo dd if=~/Downloads/ivyrain.iso of=/dev/sdb status=progress bs=1M oflag=sync
3. I rebooted my device
4. Proceeded to ensure that my bios settings for secure boot and csm support were off and UEFI only was on.
5. You need to ensure the flash drive is plugged into the right side usb drive (it didnt work on the left side for some reason)
6. Once rebooted, the flash should pick up and prompt you to continue
7. Continue forward, when prompted with install custom bios, press '1', if you have the 1080p mod, press the option for x330.
8. Reboot, observe your changes in your BIOS/UEFI

Oh and disable Intel ME aha

Now I'm thinking about organising a AX210 wireless nic card for my Thinkpad X230, as the wireless nic in it currently is really old and the bluetooth is busted.
I may also consider organising a X230 FHD kit, costs about $80 for the kit and an additional $100 for a FHD screen but I think it would be worth it considering the device is still really handy. Old tech but the thing is a tank.

*Note* For me I lost my arch linux boot so I had to sudo mount the partitions and re-configure GRUB.
